Through this skill, I developed an understanding of how an interactive command-line interface works and how a shell continuously communicates with the user. I learned how to design a main loop that repeatedly displays a prompt, waits for user input, processes the entered command, and then returns to the prompt for the next command. I understood that the main loop is the basic control structure of an interactive shell because it keeps the program running until the user specifically chooses to exit. I also learned how to design the control flow of the shell by identifying the different stages involved, such as displaying the prompt, accepting input, checking the entered command, processing it, and deciding whether to continue or terminate the program.
I learned how to display a command prompt that indicates to the user that the shell is ready to accept input. After displaying the prompt, the program needs to capture the input provided by the user and store it in an input buffer. I understood the importance of managing this buffer properly because commands can contain multiple characters and the shell needs to preserve the complete input before processing it. I also learned how to handle the Enter key as an indication that the user has finished entering a command. Once Enter is detected, the collected characters can be treated as a complete command and passed to the appropriate processing stage.
Another important part of this skill was learning how keyboard input is handled at a lower level. Instead of treating the entire command as a single input, I understood that keyboard input can be received character by character. This makes it possible for the shell to respond to special keys such as Enter and Backspace. I learned how Backspace should be handled by removing the most recently entered character from the input buffer and updating the display so that the user can correct typing mistakes. This helped me understand that even simple features such as editing a command line require proper input-buffer management and control over the terminal interface.
I also developed the ability to handle multi-character commands and understand how individual characters are combined to form a complete command. For example, when a user enters a command such as hello or ls, each character is captured and stored sequentially until the Enter key is pressed. The input buffer therefore acts as temporary storage for the command before it is processed. I learned that the buffer must also be managed carefully to avoid unnecessary characters, incorrect input, or overflow. This gave me a better understanding of how command-line interfaces process user input before passing it to other parts of the shell.
Finally, I learned how to test the interactive behaviour of the shell by entering different commands, using Backspace to correct input, pressing Enter to submit commands, and testing the exit condition to terminate the program. I understood that the shell must maintain a continuous interaction with the user while also responding correctly to special input conditions. Overall, this skill helped me understand the basic working of an interactive shell, including its main control loop, prompt handling, keyboard input, input buffering, Backspace processing, Enter-key handling, multi-character commands, and user interaction. These concepts form the foundation for developing a simple command-line shell and can later be extended with features such as command execution, process creation, built-in commands, and system-call handling.
